<article class="env-lemma" id="066J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/066J">Lemma <span data-tag="066J">15.68.11</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a ring map. Assume that $B$ is flat as an $A$-module. Let $K^\bullet $ be a complex of $B$-modules. Let $a, b \in \mathbf{Z}$. If $K^\bullet $ as a complex of $B$-modules has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$, then $K^\bullet $ as a complex of $A$-modules has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is a special case of Lemma <a href="/tag/0B66" data-tag="0B66">15.68.10</a>, but can also be seen directly as follows. We have $K^\bullet \otimes _ A^{\mathbf{L}} M = K^\bullet \otimes _ B^{\mathbf{L}} (M \otimes _ A B)$ since any projective resolution of $K^\bullet $ as a complex of $B$-modules is a flat resolution of $K^\bullet $ as a complex of $A$-modules and can be used to compute $K^\bullet \otimes _ A^{\mathbf{L}} M$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>